//? Title: IDX Loading: Shape Headers and Paired Rows
//? Subtitle: Big-endian dimensions describe bytes; normalisation is a separate operation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A tensor file must describe its dtype and shape accurately before its values can be trusted.
We write a two-image IDX fixture with known bytes, load it through the generic reader, and
check normalisation and matched image/label gathering. Damaged file lengths test the boundary
between a valid tensor and an incomplete payload.
)
\toc

> The method

An IDX header starts with two zero bytes, a type byte and a rank byte, followed by one
big-endian 32-bit length per axis. Unsigned-byte rank-three image magic is 2051; rank-one
label magic is 2049. If dimensions are d1..dr and element width is b, the complete file needs

$$
\mathrm{bytes}=4+4r+b\prod_{j=1}^r d_j,\qquad x_{\rm model}=\mathrm{cast}(x_{\rm byte})/255.
$$
\alttext(The header contains four fixed bytes and four bytes per dimension, followed by the exact product of dimensions times element width.)

read_idx returns one tensor, widening byte types to Int32. Generic tensor decoding does not
establish a supervised dataset contract: the caller must check image rank, nonzero dimensions,
matching image/label counts, label range and any required spatial shape. load_mnist supplies
fixed-name 28 by 28 convenience loading. The raw reader accepts gzip by magic bytes. An NHWC
channel axis may be added after loading; the same row permutation must gather both tensors.
Reading and copying cost O(number of elements).

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/idx_loader_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/idx_loader_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/idx_loader_concept.tikz > build/idx_loader_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/idx_loader_concept.svg build/idx_loader_concept.dvi > build/idx_loader_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/idx_loader_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/idx_loader_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/idx_loader_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Big-endian dimensions describe bytes; normalisation is a separate operation)
\alttext(IDX header; type, rank, dimensions Then Exact tensor payload; row-major values Then Paired gather + cast; images and labels.)

> Simple examples

Two 2 by 3 images contain bytes increasing and decreasing by 51; both normalised means are .5. Their labels are 7 and 3. The file is generated locally by Python so the writer is independent of the C++ reader.

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/array_io.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 n::write_idx("build/idx_images",n::tensor({0,51,102,153,204,255,255,204,153,102,51,0},{2,2,3}).to(n::DType::Int32),true);
 n::write_idx("build/idx_labels",n::tensor({7,3}).to(n::DType::Int32),true);
 std::print("Wrote two unsigned-byte images and labels with big-endian shape headers.\n");
}
```
// result_begin:
// Wrote two unsigned-byte images and labels with big-endian shape headers.
// result_end

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/idx_loader_rows.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 const auto x=n::read_idx("build/idx_images"),y=n::read_idx("build/idx_labels");
 const auto shuffled=n::cast(n::take_rows(x,{1,0}),n::DType::Float64)/255.;
 const auto labels=n::take_rows(y,{1,0});
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.lines({0,1,2,3,4,5},n::take_rows(shuffled,{0}).to_vector<double>(),"label 3",{37,99,235})
 .lines({0,1,2,3,4,5},n::take_rows(shuffled,{1}).to_vector<double>(),"label 7",{234,88,12})
 .title("One permutation keeps images and labels paired").x_label("pixel index").y_label("byte / 255");
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");p.size(820,420).save_svg("build/idx_loader_rows.svg");
 dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;t.add_column("label",labels.to_vector<double>());t.add_column("image_mean",n::mean(shuffled,{1,2}).to_vector<double>());
 if(x.shape()!=n::Shape({2,2,3})||labels.at({0})!=3||shuffled.at({0,0,0})!=1)return 1;
 std::print("{}\n",t.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| label | image_mean |
| ---: | ---: |
| 3 | 0.5 |
| 7 | 0.5 |

\image(figures/idx_loader_rows.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Row gathering changes order while preserving the label attached to each image.)
\alttext(Row gathering changes order while preserving the label attached to each image.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;namespace d=n::data;
 const auto original=d::ReadBinary("build/idx_images");int bad=0;
 for(int mode=0;mode<3;++mode){auto b=original;if(mode==0)b.pop_back();if(mode==1)b.push_back(0);if(mode==2)b[0]=1;
 d::WriteBinary("build/idx_bad",b);try{n::read_idx("build/idx_bad");}catch(const std::exception&){++bad;}}
 if(bad!=3||original.size()!=4+4*3+12)return 1;
 std::print("Header 16 bytes + payload 12 bytes = {} bytes.\n\nTruncated, trailing and bad-magic fixtures rejected: {}.\n",original.size(),bad);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Header 16 bytes + payload 12 bytes = 28 bytes.

Truncated, trailing and bad-magic fixtures rejected: 3.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The shape header, exact byte count and pixel normalisation agree with independent struct-written
files. Paired selection is part of the dataset contract, separate from tensor decoding. This small
fixture does not claim to evaluate a recogniser or require MNIST to be downloaded.

> References

- [LeCun et al., MNIST and the IDX file format](https://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/).
